  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1123 AM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nTAIL END OF THE MOISTURE:\n\nA low pressure will track just north of the area area today and \ntonight. This low is providing several rounds of forcing, which with \nmoisture in place has resulted in showers and storms. Those chances \ncontinue through tonight as the low clips the northern CWA. During \nthe afternoon shower coverage will be highest in the eastern tow \nthirds of the area and tonight the highest chances will be along and \nnorth of I-70. Marginal shear will be present once again this \nafternoon so that along with cape around 500-1000 j/kg a few \nstorms may be strong to severe. The main threats would be large \nhail and damaging winds. Given PWAT values are still 150 percent\nof normal and saturated soil there is a potential for heavy \nrainfall and therefore flash flooding. \n\n\nDRYING OUT:\n\nThe low pressure tracks to our east tomorrow and dry air moves in \nfrom the northwest. There may still be enough lingering moisture for \nshowers mainly over the higher terrain, but those chances are low. \nThe same can be said for Monday as well.  \n\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES RETURN:\n\nThe next system of interest will be a low pressure that makes \nlandfall in California. It will progress eastward and tap into the \nmoisture that will be shunted to our south tomorrow. The low is \nexpected to become an open wave and move through the area relatively \nfast. The result from the wave will be scattered showers and storms \nacross the area on Tuesday and Wednesday at the least. The wave will \nprovide shear so some of the storms could be strong to severe. Also, \nfreezing levels may come down enough to coat the highest peaks with \nsnow. The uncertainty is will the wave push the moisture to our\nsouth or will it linger in portions of the area. If it does get\npushed southward how quick will it return. Confidence in the\nforecast mid to late week is low attm.",
